1
00:00:00,106 --> 00:00:01,116
How big is an error?

2
00:00:01,542 --> 00:00:04,827
How far apart are two words inside a language model?

3
00:00:05,252 --> 00:00:07,300
Is one training step too large?

4
00:00:07,699 --> 00:00:10,811
Machine learning asks questions like these all the time.

5
00:00:11,236 --> 00:00:15,066
Each answer is a single number that measures the size of a vector.

6
00:00:15,492 --> 00:00:19,095
That number is called the norm, and it is the topic of this video.

7
00:00:20,039 --> 00:00:26,914
You will learn to compute the length of any vector, in two dimensions, in three, or in a million.

8
00:00:27,353 --> 00:00:35,066
You will meet three different ways to measure size, the rules that every norm must follow, and how distance comes from length.

9
00:00:35,491 --> 00:00:40,026
You need video one, on vectors, and video five, on the inner product.

10
00:00:41,726 --> 00:00:43,534
Let's start with the simplest question.

11
00:00:43,973 --> 00:00:46,101
How long is this blue arrow?

12
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:49,412
It goes three steps to the right and four steps up.

13
00:00:49,864 --> 00:00:53,202
The two steps and the arrow make a right triangle.

14
00:00:53,627 --> 00:00:56,513
The arrow is the long side of that triangle.

15
00:00:57,452 --> 00:00:59,434
Pythagoras tells us the answer.

16
00:00:59,873 --> 00:01:03,769
The long side squared equals three squared plus four squared.

17
00:01:04,248 --> 00:01:05,458
Three squared is nine.

18
00:01:05,937 --> 00:01:07,599
Four squared is sixteen.

19
00:01:08,011 --> 00:01:10,378
Nine plus sixteen is twenty five.

20
00:01:10,817 --> 00:01:13,769
The square root of twenty five is five.

21
00:01:14,208 --> 00:01:15,990
So the arrow has length five.

22
00:01:16,926 --> 00:01:21,753
Now look at the sum we computed: three times three, plus four times four.

23
00:01:22,192 --> 00:01:26,288
That is the dot product of the vector with itself, from video five.

24
00:01:26,713 --> 00:01:30,264
So the length is the square root of x dot x.

25
00:01:30,689 --> 00:01:33,043
This length is called the norm of x.

26
00:01:33,482 --> 00:01:35,609
We write it with double bars around x.

27
00:01:37,139 --> 00:01:39,254
Let's read the formula symbol by symbol.

28
00:01:39,679 --> 00:01:41,740
The double bars mean the norm of x.

29
00:01:42,073 --> 00:01:46,421
Inside the square root, we square each component and add them all.

30
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:50,597
The small letter i counts the components, from one up to n.

31
00:01:51,022 --> 00:01:54,932
In two dimensions this is exactly Pythagoras.

32
00:01:55,371 --> 00:01:58,668
But the same recipe works in any number of dimensions.

33
00:02:00,206 --> 00:02:02,028
Let's try three dimensions.

34
00:02:02,467 --> 00:02:05,711
Take x equal to two, three, six.

35
00:02:06,150 --> 00:02:10,445
Square each component: four, nine, and thirty six.

36
00:02:10,871 --> 00:02:12,254
Add them: forty nine.

37
00:02:12,679 --> 00:02:15,392
The square root of forty nine is seven.

38
00:02:15,831 --> 00:02:18,424
So this arrow in space has length seven.

39
00:02:18,849 --> 00:02:23,530
With a million components, a computer does exactly the same steps.

40
00:02:25,046 --> 00:02:26,176
Where does the norm come from?

41
00:02:26,588 --> 00:02:30,498
It comes from the inner product, which is axiom B in this series.

42
00:02:30,924 --> 00:02:34,780
The inner product gives a vector space lengths and angles.

43
00:02:35,232 --> 00:02:36,934
The norm is the length part.

44
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:42,785
This standard norm has two more names: the L two norm, and the Euclidean norm.

45
00:02:44,506 --> 00:02:48,362
But is the straight line the only sensible way to measure size?

46
00:02:48,788 --> 00:02:52,259
Imagine a taxi in a city with a grid of streets.

47
00:02:52,697 --> 00:02:54,679
It cannot drive through the buildings.

48
00:02:55,091 --> 00:02:59,665
To reach the point three, four, it drives three blocks right and four blocks up.

49
00:03:00,078 --> 00:03:02,139
That is seven blocks, not five.

50
00:03:03,073 --> 00:03:06,596
This gives the L one norm, also called the Manhattan norm.

51
00:03:07,035 --> 00:03:09,469
Add the absolute values of the components.

52
00:03:09,908 --> 00:03:14,815
The absolute value removes the minus sign, so minus three counts as three.

53
00:03:15,253 --> 00:03:20,839
For the vector three, four, the L one norm is three plus four, which is seven.

54
00:03:21,759 --> 00:03:24,605
A third way is to keep only the biggest component.

55
00:03:25,031 --> 00:03:28,661
This is the L infinity norm, also called the max norm.

56
00:03:29,086 --> 00:03:31,493
It takes the largest absolute value.

57
00:03:31,985 --> 00:03:34,312
For three, four, that is four.

58
00:03:34,725 --> 00:03:39,671
So the same vector has three sizes: seven, five, and four.

59
00:03:40,097 --> 00:03:44,366
They measure different things, and all three are real norms.

60
00:03:45,932 --> 00:03:49,057
Here is a beautiful way to see the difference.

61
00:03:49,456 --> 00:03:52,967
Draw every vector whose norm is exactly one.

62
00:03:53,419 --> 00:03:56,544
For the L two norm, you get the familiar circle.

63
00:03:56,983 --> 00:03:58,991
For the L one norm, you get a diamond.

64
00:03:59,443 --> 00:04:01,877
For the L infinity norm, you get a square.

65
00:04:02,302 --> 00:04:04,483
Each norm has its own unit circle.

66
00:04:05,406 --> 00:04:06,137
One honest note.

67
00:04:06,576 --> 00:04:12,241
Only the L two norm comes from an inner product, as the square root of x dot x.

68
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:18,690
The L one and L infinity norms are real norms, but no inner product gives them.

69
00:04:19,116 --> 00:04:23,145
This is a known fact, proved with a test called the parallelogram law.

70
00:04:23,557 --> 00:04:26,363
So these norms give sizes, but no angles.

71
00:04:28,106 --> 00:04:29,263
What makes a rule a norm?

72
00:04:29,688 --> 00:04:31,922
Every norm must follow three rules.

73
00:04:32,348 --> 00:04:34,981
Rule one: the norm is never negative.

74
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:37,853
And it is zero only for the zero vector.

75
00:04:38,279 --> 00:04:41,537
Any arrow that really goes somewhere has a positive length.

76
00:04:43,059 --> 00:04:44,881
Rule two is about scaling.

77
00:04:45,307 --> 00:04:51,370
Multiply a vector by a number c, and its norm is multiplied by the absolute value of c.

78
00:04:51,995 --> 00:04:55,134
Watch: two times the vector doubles the length.

79
00:04:55,546 --> 00:05:00,918
Minus two times the vector flips the arrow, but the length is still doubled.

80
00:05:01,331 --> 00:05:06,663
Minus two times three, four is minus six, minus eight, with norm ten.

81
00:05:08,206 --> 00:05:10,759
Rule three is the triangle inequality.

82
00:05:11,185 --> 00:05:13,512
Walk along u, and then along v.

83
00:05:13,937 --> 00:05:15,626
Or walk straight to u plus v.

84
00:05:16,052 --> 00:05:19,084
The straight route is never longer than the detour.

85
00:05:19,496 --> 00:05:24,788
In symbols, the norm of u plus v is at most the norm of u plus the norm of v.

86
00:05:25,739 --> 00:05:26,843
Let's check it with numbers.

87
00:05:27,268 --> 00:05:32,601
Take u equal to three, four, and v equal to four, minus three.

88
00:05:33,013 --> 00:05:36,457
Each has norm five, so the detour is ten.

89
00:05:36,909 --> 00:05:38,372
Their sum is seven, one.

90
00:05:38,798 --> 00:05:44,356
Its norm is the square root of fifty, about seven point zero seven.

91
00:05:44,782 --> 00:05:46,803
That is less than ten, as promised.

92
00:05:48,539 --> 00:05:51,824
Sometimes we care only about direction, not about length.

93
00:05:52,263 --> 00:05:56,877
Then we use a unit vector: a vector whose norm is exactly one.

94
00:05:57,302 --> 00:06:02,076
Every unit vector in the plane has its tip on the unit circle that we drew before.

95
00:06:04,006 --> 00:06:08,487
To turn any vector into a unit vector, divide it by its own norm.

96
00:06:08,913 --> 00:06:10,642
This is called normalizing.

97
00:06:11,067 --> 00:06:13,554
Take x equal to three, four.

98
00:06:13,993 --> 00:06:15,056
Its norm is five.

99
00:06:15,469 --> 00:06:20,455
Divide each component by five: zero point six and zero point eight.

100
00:06:20,894 --> 00:06:22,862
The direction stays the same.

101
00:06:23,274 --> 00:06:24,724
Only the length changes.

102
00:06:25,639 --> 00:06:27,833
Let's check that the new length is one.

103
00:06:28,272 --> 00:06:31,956
Zero point six squared is zero point three six.

104
00:06:32,381 --> 00:06:35,812
Zero point eight squared is zero point six four.

105
00:06:36,238 --> 00:06:40,586
The sum is exactly one, and the square root of one is one.

106
00:06:41,012 --> 00:06:46,238
It works because of rule two: dividing by five divides the length by five.

107
00:06:47,773 --> 00:06:49,302
Be careful with one case.

108
00:06:49,741 --> 00:06:54,315
The zero vector has norm zero, and we cannot divide by zero.

109
00:06:54,754 --> 00:06:58,983
So the zero vector has no direction, and it cannot be normalized.

110
00:06:59,408 --> 00:07:02,719
Normalizing is everywhere in machine learning.

111
00:07:03,198 --> 00:07:09,913
For example, cosine similarity from video five is the dot product of two normalized vectors.

112
00:07:11,639 --> 00:07:13,780
Now, how far apart are two points?

113
00:07:14,219 --> 00:07:19,086
Take p equal to one, one, and q equal to four, five.

114
00:07:19,512 --> 00:07:24,379
The arrow from p to q is q minus p, which is three, four.

115
00:07:24,804 --> 00:07:27,770
The distance is the length of that arrow: five.

116
00:07:28,222 --> 00:07:31,426
So distance is simply the norm of the difference.

117
00:07:32,352 --> 00:07:33,935
Here is the formula.

118
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:38,443
The distance d between x and y is the norm of x minus y.

119
00:07:38,855 --> 00:07:47,206
In three dimensions, take x equal to one, two, three, and y equal to minus one, minus one, zero.

120
00:07:47,632 --> 00:07:50,092
The difference is two, three, three.

121
00:07:50,531 --> 00:07:53,230
The squares are four, nine, and nine.

122
00:07:53,656 --> 00:07:58,350
The distance is the square root of twenty two, about four point six nine.

123
00:07:59,906 --> 00:08:02,619
A distance rule like this is called a metric.

124
00:08:03,031 --> 00:08:05,252
Every metric follows three rules.

125
00:08:05,690 --> 00:08:11,049
The distance is never negative, and it is zero only when the two points are the same.

126
00:08:11,488 --> 00:08:15,571
The distance from x to y equals the distance from y to x.

127
00:08:15,996 --> 00:08:19,268
And a detour through a third point is never shorter.

128
00:08:20,806 --> 00:08:23,479
These rules come straight from the three norm rules.

129
00:08:23,904 --> 00:08:24,915
Notice one more thing.

130
00:08:25,327 --> 00:08:29,875
Distance and the inner product move in opposite directions.

131
00:08:30,314 --> 00:08:34,622
Two very similar vectors have a large inner product, but a small distance.

132
00:08:35,061 --> 00:08:38,000
Keep this in mind when you read about similarity search.

133
00:08:39,739 --> 00:08:42,000
Now let's see norms at work in machine learning.

134
00:08:42,465 --> 00:08:44,234
First, regularization.

135
00:08:44,659 --> 00:08:49,234
Very large weights often make a model fit the noise in its training data.

136
00:08:49,659 --> 00:08:54,673
So we add a penalty to the loss, the error score that we study in video fifteen.

137
00:08:55,098 --> 00:09:00,231
The penalty is lambda times the squared L two norm of the weights.

138
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:02,997
Now the model prefers small weights.

139
00:09:04,526 --> 00:09:08,954
If we use the L one norm instead, something special happens.

140
00:09:09,393 --> 00:09:16,840
The L one penalty pushes many weights to exactly zero, so the model uses only a few inputs.

141
00:09:17,265 --> 00:09:18,529
This is called sparsity.

142
00:09:18,941 --> 00:09:24,792
Look at the diamond: its sharp corners sit on the axes, where one weight is zero.

143
00:09:25,204 --> 00:09:31,068
Ridge regression uses the L two penalty, and lasso uses the L one penalty.

144
00:09:32,619 --> 00:09:34,321
Second, gradient clipping.

145
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:39,069
During training, the gradient tells the model how to change its weights.

146
00:09:39,481 --> 00:09:41,196
We will study it in video eight.

147
00:09:41,635 --> 00:09:46,768
Sometimes the gradient becomes huge, and one bad step can ruin the training.

148
00:09:47,207 --> 00:09:49,055
So we measure its L two norm.

149
00:09:49,468 --> 00:09:53,430
If the norm is larger than a maximum, we scale the gradient down.

150
00:09:54,373 --> 00:09:55,702
Here is a toy example.

151
00:09:56,141 --> 00:10:02,205
Say the gradient is six, eight, with norm ten, and the maximum norm is five.

152
00:10:02,644 --> 00:10:06,846
We multiply by five divided by ten, which is one half.

153
00:10:07,285 --> 00:10:12,418
The new gradient is three, four, and its norm is exactly five.

154
00:10:12,843 --> 00:10:14,133
The direction is kept.

155
00:10:14,572 --> 00:10:16,367
Only the length is capped.

156
00:10:16,779 --> 00:10:20,131
This is a standard trick when training large language models.

157
00:10:21,712 --> 00:10:23,641
Third, nearest neighbour search.

158
00:10:24,093 --> 00:10:27,484
Here are three toy data points and a new query point.

159
00:10:27,923 --> 00:10:28,920
Which one is closest?

160
00:10:29,332 --> 00:10:31,008
Compute the distance to each.

161
00:10:31,433 --> 00:10:33,534
A is about three point one six away.

162
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:35,742
B is about two point two four.

163
00:10:36,181 --> 00:10:37,803
C is about two point eight three.

164
00:10:38,255 --> 00:10:40,024
So B is the nearest neighbour.

165
00:10:40,449 --> 00:10:44,731
Search over word vectors works the same way, with millions of points.

166
00:10:46,439 --> 00:10:47,304
Now it is your turn.

167
00:10:47,742 --> 00:10:49,577
Pause the video and try this.

168
00:10:50,016 --> 00:10:52,503
Take the vector minus five, twelve.

169
00:10:52,942 --> 00:10:54,059
What is its L two norm?

170
00:10:54,484 --> 00:10:55,694
And what is its L one norm?

171
00:10:56,120 --> 00:11:01,320
Remember: for L two, square, add, and take the square root.

172
00:11:01,798 --> 00:11:04,445
For L one, add the absolute values.

173
00:11:05,386 --> 00:11:06,370
Here are the answers.

174
00:11:06,795 --> 00:11:08,936
Minus five squared is twenty five.

175
00:11:09,375 --> 00:11:11,662
Twelve squared is one hundred forty four.

176
00:11:12,088 --> 00:11:16,769
The sum is one hundred sixty nine, and its square root is thirteen.

177
00:11:17,274 --> 00:11:20,825
For L one, five plus twelve is seventeen.

178
00:11:21,250 --> 00:11:24,415
The taxi route is never shorter than the straight line.

179
00:11:25,939 --> 00:11:26,750
One more question.

180
00:11:27,163 --> 00:11:31,378
What is the distance between the points two, seven and five, three?

181
00:11:31,804 --> 00:11:36,338
Then, normalize the difference vector, so that its length is one.

182
00:11:36,764 --> 00:11:37,615
Pause and think.

183
00:11:38,592 --> 00:11:39,404
First subtract.

184
00:11:39,842 --> 00:11:44,816
Two minus five is minus three, and seven minus three is four.

185
00:11:45,241 --> 00:11:49,696
The norm of minus three, four is five, so the distance is five.

186
00:11:50,135 --> 00:11:52,209
To normalize, divide by five.

187
00:11:52,635 --> 00:11:56,039
You get minus zero point six, and zero point eight.

188
00:11:56,465 --> 00:11:57,954
Its length is one.

189
00:11:59,673 --> 00:12:02,359
Let's check everything in Python with NumPy.

190
00:12:02,798 --> 00:12:07,970
The function norm, from the linear algebra part of NumPy, computes norms.

191
00:12:08,396 --> 00:12:13,609
The argument ord chooses which one: one, two, or infinity.

192
00:12:14,048 --> 00:12:20,298
We also normalize a vector, compute a distance, clip a gradient, and find a nearest neighbour.

193
00:12:21,839 --> 00:12:25,669
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

194
00:12:26,148 --> 00:12:29,765
Three, four has norms seven, five, and four.

195
00:12:30,190 --> 00:12:32,970
Two, three, six has norm seven.

196
00:12:33,395 --> 00:12:37,185
The unit vector is zero point six, zero point eight.

197
00:12:37,624 --> 00:12:39,884
The distance is four point six nine.

198
00:12:40,323 --> 00:12:45,031
The clipped gradient is three, four, and the nearest neighbour is B.

199
00:12:46,773 --> 00:12:47,384
In summary.

200
00:12:47,823 --> 00:12:50,882
The norm measures the size of a vector as one number.

201
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:58,076
The standard L two norm is the square root of x dot x: Pythagoras in any dimension.

202
00:12:58,501 --> 00:13:04,618
The L one norm adds absolute values, and the L infinity norm takes the largest one.

203
00:13:05,031 --> 00:13:11,719
Every norm is positive, scales with the absolute value of c, and obeys the triangle inequality.

204
00:13:12,639 --> 00:13:15,033
Divide by the norm to get a unit vector.

205
00:13:15,472 --> 00:13:17,626
The norm of a difference is a distance.

206
00:13:18,025 --> 00:13:23,530
In machine learning, norms keep weights small, cap gradients, and find nearest neighbours.

207
00:13:23,942 --> 00:13:29,793
Next, in video seven, we meet the derivative: the slope of a curve right here, at one point.

208
00:13:30,219 --> 00:13:30,764
See you there.

209
00:13:32,706 --> 00:13:34,528
This video was made by David R.

210
00:13:34,967 --> 00:13:38,198
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

211
00:13:38,756 --> 00:13:39,474
Thanks for watching.
